# Audit results and detailed explanations, part B (q61-q120). Format: see explain_a.py.
E = {}
def e(qid, stem, exp, opts=None, q=None):
    E[qid] = dict(stem=stem, exp=exp, opts=opts, q=q)

e("q61", "A store sets its spending plan", "A static budget is set for one expected level of activity and never changes. When sales run 20% higher, costs like materials and staffing naturally rise too, so comparing them to the old fixed numbers makes managers look like they overspent. A flexible budget adjusts the plan to the actual activity level, making the comparison fair.")
e("q62", "The main purpose of the financial-information", "Financial-information management collects, records, analyzes and reports a company's financial data so managers have accurate numbers for decisions like budget allocation, pricing and investment. Selling products is marketing and hiring is HR, and good information supports budgets rather than replacing them.")
e("q63", "A company compares its profit margins", "Benchmarking measures a company's performance against competitors or industry standards to see where it's ahead or behind. If a rival's costs are lower, benchmarking points to where to improve. Normalization organizes database data, and stewardship means responsible management of resources.")
e("q64", "After two large companies merge", "When companies merge, each brings its own accounting software, chart of accounts and data formats. Combining them into one reliable system is slow and error-prone, which is why mergers and acquisitions often disrupt financial-information management for months.")
e("q65", "Almaz, a financial analyst", "Reviewing a vendor her brother owns is a conflict of interest: her personal relationship could bias, or appear to bias, her judgment. The ethical response is to disclose the connection to her manager and step away, so someone impartial does the review. Keeping it secret or rating it favorably would be a serious ethics breach.")
e("q66", "A record that tracks every step", "An audit trail is a step-by-step record showing who entered, approved and changed each transaction, and when. It lets accountants and auditors trace any number back to its source, which makes errors and fraud easier to find. A balance sheet shows totals, not the history of each transaction.")
e("q67", "Ensuring a company's financial resources", "Stewardship means managing resources that belong to others, such as shareholders, donors or customers, responsibly and ethically to serve the organization's mission. Leverage is the use of debt, convergence is combining financial services, and normalization is a database technique.")
e("q68", "Why does ethical financial reporting", "Investors and lenders provide money only when they trust the numbers. Honest, accurate reporting builds that trust, which lowers the company's cost of raising capital. Misleading reports, as at Enron and WorldCom, destroy trust and can sink a company. Ethics doesn't guarantee sales or replace audits.")
e("q69", "A business moves its financial records", "Cloud systems can be reached from anywhere over the internet, which is convenient but also gives hackers more ways in. The biggest new risk is a cybersecurity breach, so strong passwords, multifactor authentication and encryption become essential. Fire risk to paper records actually goes down.")
e("q70", "What is the main benefit of automated data", "Automation pulls data directly from banks, invoices and point-of-sale systems instead of having people type it in. That cuts errors like typos and transposed numbers and frees accountants to analyze instead of enter data. It doesn't eliminate accountants or guarantee profit.")
e("q71", "A program that blocks unauthorized users", "A firewall sits between a company's network and the internet, checking traffic against security rules and blocking anything unauthorized. It's a basic cybersecurity defense for financial systems. Spreadsheets, queries and ledgers store or analyze data but don't protect the network.")
e("q72", "Which ratio shows whether a company can pay", "The acid-test (quick) ratio = (current assets - inventory) / current liabilities. It leaves out inventory because inventory may take time to sell, so it tests whether the company can pay bills right away from cash, securities and receivables. A ratio of 1.0 or higher is generally considered safe.")
e("q73", "An analyst lists each income statement item", "Vertical (common-size) analysis expresses each line as a percentage of a base figure, here total revenue. That shows the cost structure, such as COGS being 60% of sales, and allows comparison with companies of different sizes. Horizontal analysis compares the same item across years.")
e("q74", "A supplier reviews a new business customer's", "Credit standards are the rules a business uses to decide which customers can buy now and pay later, based on credit reports, payment history and financial ratios. Checking them before offering 30-day terms protects the supplier from customers who might not pay.")
e("q75", "Why would an investor calculate a company's debt", "Debt-to-equity = total liabilities / shareholders' equity. It shows how much the company relies on borrowed money. High debt means large required interest payments, which raises the risk of trouble in a downturn, so investors use it to judge financial risk.")
e("q76", "An auditor compares employee home addresses", "If an employee's address matches a vendor's address, the employee may have created a fake vendor and be paying themselves, or may secretly own a real vendor. Matching employee and vendor data is a standard fraud-detection test in accounts payable.")
e("q77", "Comparing this year's sales to the last five", "Horizontal (trend) analysis compares the same line item across several periods to see growth or decline, for example sales rising 8% a year. Vertical analysis compares items within one period as percentages of a base.")
e("q78", "A key benefit of budgeting software", "Budgeting software lets managers change assumptions, like sales growth, prices or interest rates, and instantly see the effect on the whole budget. Running many what-if scenarios helps them prepare for different outcomes. No software can eliminate expenses or guarantee a forecast.")
e("q79", "Which budget tracks the cash expected", "A cash flow (cash) budget forecasts when cash will come in and go out, month by month. Its key use is spotting future shortfalls early, so the business can arrange financing or delay spending before it runs out of cash. A capital budget plans long-term asset purchases.")
e("q80", "A company asks each department manager to build", "In bottom-up budgeting, the people closest to the work estimate their own needs, and those estimates are combined into the company budget. It tends to be more accurate and gives managers ownership. Top-down budgeting is set by senior leaders and handed down.")
e("q81", "A quality method focused on maximizing customer", "Lean, which grew out of the Toyota Production System, maximizes value for the customer by eliminating waste, meaning any step, inventory or motion that doesn't add value. Financial data helps identify where waste costs the most.")
e("q82", "A rental company switches to activity-based", "Activity-based costing assigns overhead to each product or service by the activities it actually uses, such as cleaning, inspections and deliveries, instead of spreading it evenly. That shows the true cost and profit of each rental, revealing which ones really make money.")
e("q83", "A report on a company's environmental", "A sustainability report discloses environmental, social and governance (ESG) results, such as emissions, energy use and workforce data, along with the costs and risks of issues like climate change. It's a non-traditional use of financial information.")
e("q84", "Six Sigma primarily helps a business", "Six Sigma uses statistical data and the DMAIC process (define, measure, analyze, improve, control) to reduce variation and defects, aiming for no more than 3.4 defects per million opportunities. Fewer defects mean lower costs and higher quality.")
e("q85", "Which is an example of unethical risk", "Hiding a safety flaw in the fine print deceives customers to shift risk onto them. It's unethical, and it usually backfires with lawsuits and reputation damage. Buying insurance, training employees and backing up data are legitimate, ethical ways to manage risk.")
e("q86", "A manufacturer switches to cheaper", "Lower-quality materials raise the chance that products fail or hurt someone, which leads to recalls, liability lawsuits and a damaged reputation. Interest-rate, currency and inflation risks come from the economy, not from material choices.")
e("q87", "A bank uses machine learning to review", "Machine learning models learn what normal transactions look like for each customer and flag ones that don't fit, such as an unusual location or amount, for review. They can check millions of transactions in real time, far beyond what human reviewers could do.")
e("q88", "Simulating many possible outcomes", "Risk modeling uses historical data, probabilities and simulations to estimate how likely and how large possible losses are. Managers use the results to set limits, buy insurance or hold reserves. Risk avoidance means not doing the risky activity at all.")
e("q89", "Which practice best protects a business from data", "Regular backups stored off-site (or in a separate cloud location) mean that if a fire or outage destroys the main systems, the data can be restored. A shared password weakens security, printing everything is impractical, and disabling antivirus increases risk.")
e("q90", "A parent company forms its own insurance", "A captive insurance company is owned by the business it insures. The parent pays premiums to its own insurer, which can lower costs, cover unusual risks and keep underwriting profits in the company. It's a legal way to transfer and finance risk.")
e("q91", "Which law requires public companies to establish", "The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 was passed after the Enron and WorldCom scandals. Section 404 requires public companies to assess and report on internal controls over financial reporting, and CEOs and CFOs must certify the statements. The other laws cover lending, pollution and wages.")
e("q92", "Requiring two employees to approve any payment", "Dual approval is an internal control: a rule created inside the company to prevent and detect errors and fraud. No single person can send a large payment alone, so stealing requires collusion. It isn't an external risk, avoidance or speculation.")
e("q93", "Which risk is external and largely", "Inflation is a macroeconomic condition driven by the whole economy and the Federal Reserve, so a single business can't control it, only respond to it. Employee theft, poor training and weak passwords are internal risks the company can reduce with its own controls.")
e("q94", "A company that implements and monitors safety", "Risk control means taking action to reduce how often losses happen and how severe they are. Safety policies, training and inspections in a warehouse lower injury risk. Risk transfer would be buying insurance, and risk retention would be accepting the risk and paying losses yourself.")
e("q95", "What makes enterprise risk management", "Traditional risk management handles risks separately by department, for example insurance in one place and IT security in another. ERM looks at all risks together across the whole organization and ties them to strategy, so it can see how risks interact and which matter most.")
e("q96", "A listing of all of an organization's", "A risk inventory (or risk register) is the master list of the organization's critical risks. Each comes with a rating for likelihood and impact, an owner and a planned response. It's the starting point for enterprise risk management.")
e("q97", "A competitor launches a superior product", "Strategic risk threatens a company's long-term goals and market position, through things like competitors, changing customer tastes or new technology. Operational risk comes from failures in daily processes, people or systems, and hazard risk from events like fires.")
e("q98", "A limitation of enterprise risk management", "ERM depends on managers estimating how likely and how severe each risk is. If those estimates or inputs are wrong or biased, the risk picture is wrong too. It covers many risks, isn't legally required for small firms, and can't eliminate risk.")
e("q99", "A broker learns a major client is about", "Front-running is when a broker trades for their own account ahead of a client's large order, knowing the client's buying will push the price up. It uses confidential client information for personal gain and is illegal.")
e("q100", "Scammers promote a cheap stock on social", "In a pump and dump, promoters hype a cheap stock, often on social media, to 'pump' up its price, then 'dump' their shares at the inflated price. When the hype ends the price collapses and later buyers lose money. It's illegal market manipulation.")
e("q101", "An 'investment manager' pays early investors", "A Ponzi scheme pays earlier investors with money from new investors instead of real profits, and it collapses when new money dries up. In a pyramid scheme, participants earn mainly by recruiting others. Bernie Madoff ran the largest known Ponzi scheme.")
e("q102", "A scammer joins a church and convinces", "Affinity fraud targets members of a close-knit group, such as a religious, ethnic or professional community, by exploiting their trust in someone who seems to be 'one of us.' Victims often skip normal checks and are reluctant to report it.")
e("q103", "A company director sells her shares", "Insider trading is buying or selling securities based on material, non-public information. The director knew about terrible earnings before the public and sold to avoid losses, which is illegal because it's unfair to investors who didn't have that information.")
e("q104", "An investor sells one stock at a $3,000 loss", "Capital losses offset capital gains dollar for dollar. His $5,000 gain minus the $3,000 loss leaves a net gain of $2,000, so he pays tax only on $2,000. Losses beyond gains can offset up to $3,000 of other income a year.")
e("q105", "Taxes on expensive, nonessential items", "Luxury taxes are placed on high-priced items that aren't necessities, such as yachts, jewelry or very expensive cars. They raise revenue mainly from wealthier buyers. Payroll taxes are on wages, property taxes on real estate and estate taxes on wealth passed at death.")
e("q106", "Why might a corporation prefer borrowing", "Interest on debt is a tax-deductible business expense, so borrowing $1 million at 6% costs less after taxes. Dividends to stockholders are paid from after-tax profits and aren't deductible. Debt must be repaid, though, so it adds risk.")
e("q107", "A key responsibility of a financial institution's", "A compliance department's job is to make sure the institution follows laws and regulations, and preventing violations comes first, through policies, training and monitoring. It also detects and corrects problems. Sales, advertising and stock prices aren't its role.")
e("q108", "Which is a likely consequence of noncompliance", "When a firm breaks financial regulations, regulators may audit and investigate, impose fines and restrict its activities, and the news damages customer trust. TD Bank's roughly $3 billion penalty in 2024 is an example. Noncompliance never brings guaranteed profits or refunds.")
e("q109", "Financial compliance helps the overall economy", "Rules that are followed and enforced keep markets fair and institutions stable. That public trust is what makes people willing to deposit, save and invest, which channels money to productive uses. Compliance can't eliminate investment risk or end the business cycle.")
e("q110", "A main advantage of using technology for compliance", "Compliance technology (regtech) automates routine monitoring and reporting, such as screening transactions, archiving messages and filing reports. That lowers costs, reduces human error and spots patterns people might miss. Firms still must follow every law.")
e("q111", "Which group is responsible for approving", "The board of directors, elected by shareholders, oversees management and approves the company's governance policies, such as its code of conduct, committee charters and risk appetite. Customers, suppliers and entry-level employees don't set governance policy.")
e("q112", "What is the main goal of a good corporate", "Corporate governance sets up checks and accountability so a company is run responsibly for its owners and stakeholders. Its main goals are to minimize unethical behavior, reduce risk and prevent mismanagement. Maximizing bonuses or keeping shareholders uninformed is what governance protects against.")
e("q113", "When a company has poor corporate governance", "Weak governance, such as no independent board oversight, poor transparency or conflicts of interest, signals a higher risk of fraud and bad decisions. Investors respond by trusting the company less, often selling shares or demanding higher returns.")
e("q114", "Which action most directly establishes ethical", "A formal code of conduct puts ethical expectations in writing for every employee, covering conflicts of interest, gifts, confidentiality and how to report concerns. It's the foundation of an ethical culture. Prices, locations and dividends don't set ethical standards.")
e("q115", "Which body sets a corporation's overall strategic", "The board of directors sets the corporation's overall strategy and acts as a fiduciary for shareholders, protecting their investment by overseeing management. The marketing department carries out strategy, and external auditors check the financial statements.")
e("q116", "An external audit focuses on", "External auditors are independent CPAs who examine the financial statements and give an opinion on whether they're fairly presented under accounting standards. Investors rely on that opinion. Product design, schedules and staffing aren't part of an audit.")
e("q117", "Why are internal audits an important part", "Internal auditors work inside the company but report independently, often to the board's audit committee. They test whether controls and governance practices work and make sure problems get fixed. They complement, but don't replace, external audits.")
e("q118", "Which is an external mechanism that influences", "External governance mechanisms come from outside the company, such as government regulations, laws like Sarbanes-Oxley, stock exchange listing rules and independent auditors. Bylaws, internal audit and the code of conduct are internal mechanisms the company creates itself.")
e("q119", "A corporation's internal rulebook", "Bylaws are a corporation's internal rules: how directors are elected, how meetings run, how votes work and who can make which decisions. They protect shareholder rights. A prospectus describes securities for sale, and an 8-K reports major events.")
e("q120", "Which change would most likely force a company", "Going public brings new requirements: SEC reporting, Sarbanes-Oxley internal controls, independent directors and audit committees required by stock exchanges, and scrutiny from many shareholders. A private company has far fewer rules, so an IPO forces stronger governance.")
